= a
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $K=2q_0-1>0$ and parametrize the <scale factor> by
$$
y=\frac{q_0}{K}(1-\cos\theta).
$$
On the expanding branch $0<\theta<\pi$, the equation from part (ii) gives
$$
\dot y=H_0\sqrt K\cot(\theta/2),\qquad
\frac{dy}{d\theta}=\frac{q_0}{K}\sin\theta.
$$
Consequently,
$$
\frac{dt}{d\theta}
=\frac{q_0}{H_0K^{3/2}}(1-\cos\theta).
$$
Choose the origin of <cosmic time> at the <big bang>, $y=0$, $\theta=0$. <Integration> proves the <closed matter-dominated Friedmann solution>:
$$
\boxed{H_0t=q_0(2q_0-1)^{-3/2}(\theta-\sin\theta),\qquad
\frac a{a_0}=\frac{q_0}{2q_0-1}(1-\cos\theta).}
$$
The same parametrization continues through maximum expansion at $\theta=\pi$ to collapse at $\theta=2\pi$: $dt/d\theta$ remains nonnegative while $dy/d\theta$ changes sign. The maximum <scale factor> is $y_{\max}=2q_0/K$, reached at $H_0t_{\max}=\pi q_0/K^{3/2}$; the total lifetime is twice this time. The squared equation alone does not prescribe the sign of $\dot y$, so this continuation supplies the collapsing branch explicitly.

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $q_0=1/2$, the expanding <Friedmann equation> reduces to $\dot y=H_0y^{-1/2}$. Separating variables and placing the <big bang> at $t=0$ gives
$$
H_0t=\int_0^y u^{1/2}\,du=\frac23y^{3/2}.
$$
Thus the flat pressureless <Einstein-de Sitter universe> has
$$
\boxed{H_0t=\frac23\left(\frac a{a_0}\right)^{3/2},\qquad
\frac a{a_0}=\left(\frac32H_0t\right)^{2/3}.}
$$
In particular its present age is $2/(3H_0)$.

= c
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $B=1-2q_0>0$ and introduce $\psi>0$ through
$$
y=\frac{q_0}{B}(\cosh\psi-1).
$$
The expanding equation becomes
$$
\dot y=H_0\sqrt B\coth(\psi/2),\qquad
\frac{dy}{d\psi}=\frac{q_0}{B}\sinh\psi.
$$
Dividing these expressions yields
$$
\frac{dt}{d\psi}=\frac{q_0}{H_0B^{3/2}}(\cosh\psi-1).
$$
Integrating from the <big bang> at $\psi=0$ proves the <open matter-dominated Friedmann solution>:
$$
\boxed{H_0t=q_0(1-2q_0)^{-3/2}(\sinh\psi-\psi),\qquad
\frac a{a_0}=\frac{q_0}{1-2q_0}(\cosh\psi-1).}
$$
The <scale factor> grows indefinitely. At early times $y\propto t^{2/3}$, as in the matter-dominated flat case, whereas at late times $\dot y\to H_0\sqrt B$ and $y\propto t$ as curvature becomes dominant.

